From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 13:05:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] msm/dsi: keep link clocks up while display active

Re-cycling the link clocks on every DCS transfer cuts the byte/pixel clock
supply to the DSI serializer mid-frame while the MDP frame pipeline is
running, causing all-lane HS FIFO underflows. Skip the cycle when the display
is already active: host_power_on()/host_power_off() own the clocks for the
duration of the display session.

The "is the display active" decision is LATCHED in xfer_prepare() and consumed
by xfer_restore() rather than being recomputed. Recomputing it is a refcount
bug: (power_on && enabled) is written by the modeset path
(msm_dsi_host_enable/disable, msm_dsi_host_power_on/off), which does NOT hold
cmd_mutex, while an asynchronous DCS transfer -- a DSI-backlight brightness
write, DCS 0x51 -- holds cmd_mutex across the prepare/restore pair. The flag
can therefore flip between the two evaluations, in either direction:

  - transfer starts enabled (prepare SKIPS the get), modeset clears `enabled`,
    restore takes the other branch and PUTS clocks it never took. The refcount
    underflows: "dsi0_phy_pll_out_dsiclk already disabled / already unprepared".

  - transfer starts disabled (prepare TAKES the get), modeset sets `enabled`,
    restore SKIPS the put. The get leaks, so the PHY PLL stays on across
    msm_dsi_host_power_off(): "Turning OFF PHY while PLL is on". The next
    enable then finds a stale enable count on dsi0_phy_pll_out_dsiclk, skips
    the real hardware enable, and clk_pixel_set_rate() cannot reprogram the
    pixel RCG against a parent that is not ticking:
    "disp_cc_mdss_pclk0_clk_src: rcg didn't update its configuration".
    Every subsequent DCS transfer then times out with -ETIMEDOUT and the panel
    never gets its init sequence -- a permanently black screen that only a
    reboot clears.

Observed on MANGMI Pocket Max (SM8250, chipone ICNA35XX): Steam writes the
panel brightness over DCS as its UI comes up, which is exactly when gamescope
takes over and does its first modeset. Intermittent, because it needs the
brightness write to straddle the flag flip.

cmd_mutex brackets the prepare/restore pair, so a single latched flag on the
host is sufficient -- transfers cannot nest.

The latch alone is NOT enough, and dsi_host_transfer() takes dev_mutex for the
whole transfer as well. Its existing `!msm_host->power_on` test is an UNLOCKED
read of a flag that msm_dsi_host_power_off() clears under dev_mutex, so the
test can pass and the entire teardown -- host_disable(), host_power_off(),
msm_dsi_phy_disable() -- then run underneath a transfer already in flight. The
transfer's link-clock reference outlives the PHY, which is the "Turning OFF PHY
while PLL is on" above, and the panel is wedged from there. Holding dev_mutex
excludes power_on()/power_off() for the duration and re-tests power_on under
it: a modeset now waits for an in-flight DCS write instead of racing it, and a
brightness write to an unpowered host is refused instead of powering the link
up behind the modeset's back. Both locks are needed -- dev_mutex does not cover
`enabled`, which msm_dsi_host_enable/disable() write with no lock at all, so
the latch is still what keeps the refcount balanced.

MEASURED, both directions, on a MANGMI Pocket Max dev card (2026-09-07). The
race reproduces on demand in ~15 s -- hammer DCS 0x51 through the backlight
sysfs while killing the gamescope session tree so it re-modesets -- and that rig
answers the "is this import even needed" question in both directions:

  - WITHOUT this patch (upstream behaviour), it wedges on iteration 1 from the
    transfer's own link_clk_set_rate(): dsi_link_clk_set_rate_6g <-
    msm_dsi_host_xfer_prepare <- dsi_host_transfer <-
    mipi_dsi_dcs_set_display_brightness_large, straight into "rcg didn't update
    its configuration". The set_rate skip is load-bearing.

  - WITH the skip and the latch but WITHOUT dev_mutex, it also wedges on
    iteration 1, from the modeset side. That is the version that shipped in
    ota/v0.3.4 and did not fix the black screen.

Signed-off-by: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>

[ARMDeck: from Nova-Deck os-build, kernel/patches/0420 (commit 325dfde9a380),
unchanged except for context offsets. On the OnePlus 8 (command-mode panel)
every brightness step went through this path while frames streamed: Steam's
auto-dim gave bursts of "dsi_err_worker: status=4" (DSI FIFO error),
coloured flicker, and brightness writes that timed out with -110
(docs/gaming-stack.md, TODO 20).]

---
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
index 4d75529..06ab28b 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
@@ -176,6 +176,16 @@ struct msm_dsi_host {
 	bool registered;
 	bool power_on;
 	bool enabled;
+	/*
+	 * Whether THIS command transfer took the link clocks up in
+	 * msm_dsi_host_xfer_prepare() and therefore owes the matching put in
+	 * msm_dsi_host_xfer_restore(). Latched rather than recomputed: the
+	 * (power_on && enabled) state it derives from is written by the
+	 * modeset path, which does not hold cmd_mutex, so re-evaluating it in
+	 * restore() can disagree with prepare() and unbalance the refcount.
+	 * cmd_mutex brackets the prepare/restore pair, so one flag is enough.
+	 */
+	bool xfer_owns_link_clks;
 	int irq;
 };
 
@@ -1652,12 +1662,41 @@ static ssize_t dsi_host_transfer(struct mipi_dsi_host *host,
 	struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host = to_msm_dsi_host(host);
 	int ret;
 
-	if (!msg || !msm_host->power_on)
+	if (!msg)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/*
+	 * dev_mutex, and not just the power_on test: that test is unlocked, and
+	 * power_on is cleared by msm_dsi_host_power_off() from the modeset path,
+	 * which takes dev_mutex and never cmd_mutex. So the test can pass and the
+	 * whole teardown -- host_disable(), host_power_off(), msm_dsi_phy_disable()
+	 * -- then run underneath a transfer that is already in flight.
+	 *
+	 * That window is what wedges the panel. The transfer holds the link clocks
+	 * across msm_dsi_phy_disable(), which turns the PHY off with its PLL still
+	 * running ("Turning OFF PHY while PLL is on"); the next power_on() then
+	 * cannot reprogram the pixel RCG against a parent that is not ticking
+	 * ("disp_cc_mdss_pclk0_clk_src: rcg didn't update its configuration"), and
+	 * from there every DCS transfer times out at -ETIMEDOUT, the panel never
+	 * gets its init sequence, and only a reboot clears it.
+	 *
+	 * Holding dev_mutex for the transfer excludes power_on()/power_off() for
+	 * its duration and re-tests power_on under that lock. A modeset now waits
+	 * for an in-flight DCS write instead of racing it. No deadlock: the
+	 * transfer path never takes dev_mutex below this point, and the modeset
+	 * path calls into the panel (and thus back into ->transfer) only after
+	 * msm_dsi_host_power_on() has already dropped dev_mutex.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&msm_host->dev_mutex);
+	if (!msm_host->power_on) {
+		mutex_unlock(&msm_host->dev_mutex);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
 	mutex_lock(&msm_host->cmd_mutex);
 	ret = msm_dsi_manager_cmd_xfer(msm_host->id, msg);
 	mutex_unlock(&msm_host->cmd_mutex);
+	mutex_unlock(&msm_host->dev_mutex);
 
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -2053,8 +2092,22 @@ int msm_dsi_host_xfer_prepare(struct mipi_dsi_host *host,
 	 * mdp clock need to be enabled to receive dsi interrupt
 	 */
 	pm_runtime_get_sync(&msm_host->pdev->dev);
-	cfg_hnd->ops->link_clk_set_rate(msm_host);
-	cfg_hnd->ops->link_clk_enable(msm_host);
+
+	/*
+	 * Skip the link-clock cycle when the display is already active: the
+	 * clocks are owned by host_power_on()/host_power_off() for the
+	 * duration of the display session, and re-cycling them here while the
+	 * MDP frame pipeline is running cuts the byte/pixel clock supply to
+	 * the DSI serializer mid-frame, causing all-lane HS FIFO underflows.
+	 *
+	 * LATCHED for xfer_restore(); see the comment on xfer_owns_link_clks.
+	 */
+	msm_host->xfer_owns_link_clks =
+		!(msm_host->power_on && msm_host->enabled);
+	if (msm_host->xfer_owns_link_clks) {
+		cfg_hnd->ops->link_clk_set_rate(msm_host);
+		cfg_hnd->ops->link_clk_enable(msm_host);
+	}
 
 	/* TODO: vote for bus bandwidth */
 
@@ -2085,7 +2138,15 @@ void msm_dsi_host_xfer_restore(struct mipi_dsi_host *host,
 
 	/* TODO: unvote for bus bandwidth */
 
-	cfg_hnd->ops->link_clk_disable(msm_host);
+	/*
+	 * Only put the link clocks if THIS transfer took them, mirroring the
+	 * latched decision in xfer_prepare().
+	 */
+	if (msm_host->xfer_owns_link_clks) {
+		cfg_hnd->ops->link_clk_disable(msm_host);
+		msm_host->xfer_owns_link_clks = false;
+	}
+
 	pm_runtime_put(&msm_host->pdev->dev);
 }
 
